Apple today updated its U.S. <a href="https://www.apple.com/shop/trade-in">trade-in estimates</a>, raising maximum values across the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad/">iPad</a> lineup and for most Macs following <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/26/apple-announces-ipad-mini-with-oled-display/">today's announcement</a> of the new <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad-mini/">iPad mini</a>.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/07/iPad-mini-7-Review-Thumb-1-sans-text.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1056456" /><br/>
Every &zwnj;iPad&zwnj; category has gained value, although the increases are relatively modest. The &zwnj;iPad mini&zwnj; and standard &zwnj;iPad&zwnj; both rise by &#36;20, while the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad-air/">iPad Air</a> gains &#36;15 and the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad-pro/">iPad Pro</a> increases by &#36;10. The revised maximum estimates are as follows:<br/>
<br/>
<ul><br/>
<li><strong>iPad Pro</strong>: &#36;905 to &#36;915</li><br/>
<li><strong>iPad Air</strong>: &#36;510 to &#36;525</li><br/>
<li><strong>iPad mini</strong>: &#36;320 to &#36;340</li><br/>
<li><strong>iPad</strong>: &#36;280 to &#36;300</li><br/>
</ul><br/>
<br/>
The Mac sees substantially larger increases, led by the Mac Pro, which gains &#36;365 after holding at &#36;2,195 in the previous update. The <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/mac-studio/">Mac Studio</a> rises by &#36;240, while the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-pro/">MacBook Pro</a> and <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/macbook-air/">MacBook Air</a> gain &#36;120 and &#36;90, respectively:<br/>
<br/>
<ul><br/>
<li><strong>MacBook Pro</strong>: &#36;1,315 to &#36;1,435</li><br/>
<li><strong>MacBook Air</strong>: &#36;705 to &#36;795</li><br/>
<li><strong>Mac mini</strong>: &#36;620 to &#36;645</li><br/>
<li><strong>Mac Studio</strong>: &#36;1,460 to &#36;1,700</li><br/>
<li><strong>iMac</strong>: &#36;385 to &#36;440</li><br/>
<li><strong>Mac Pro</strong>: &#36;2,195 to &#36;2,560</li><br/>
</ul><br/>
<br/>
The <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/imac/">iMac</a> Pro is the only listed Mac category left unchanged, remaining at up to &#36;340. All listed iPhone and Apple Watch trade-in estimates also remain at the values <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/15/apple-cuts-iphone-trade-in-value/">from September</a>.<br/>
<br/>
The increases continue a run of higher Mac trade-in estimates this year. The &zwnj;MacBook Pro&zwnj;'s maximum value has more than doubled from &#36;690 in <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/05/27/apple-updates-trade-in-values-may-2026/">May</a> to &#36;1,435, while the &zwnj;MacBook Air&zwnj; has risen from &#36;520 to &#36;795 over the same period.<br/>
<br/>
